# Trivy findings that are accepted, with the reason.
#
# Format: one check ID per line. Add a comment above every entry explaining why
# it is safe here — an unexplained suppression is indistinguishable from a bug
# someone silenced to make CI green. Review each entry when the related
# resource changes.

# AVD-AWS-0089 — S3 bucket does not have logging enabled.
# Applies to the access log bucket itself (pointing a log bucket at itself
# creates a feedback loop, where each log write generates another log entry;
# AWS recommends against it — the site bucket and CloudFront both log into
# it), the state bucket (written only by Terraform, whose own audit trail is
# CloudTrail plus the state file's version history), and the run pipeline's
# jobs bucket (infra/modules/run-pipeline/s3-jobs.tf — every object expires
# within days by design; access logging a bucket with no durable content adds
# cost and noise for no retained signal).
AVD-AWS-0089

# AVD-AWS-0132 — S3 bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key.
# Every bucket in this repo (site, log, Terraform state, and the run
# pipeline's jobs bucket) uses SSE-S3 (AES256), which is explicitly sanctioned
# in CLAUDE.md ("AES256 (or aws:kms in prod)"). A customer-managed KMS key
# costs money per key per month and adds rotation and key-policy overhead that
# buys nothing here: the site bucket holds only public static assets served
# through CloudFront, the log bucket holds access logs (and per AWS docs,
# SSE-KMS is not even supported as a target for S3 server access logging —
# see the comment in s3-logs.tf), the state bucket's sensitivity is already
# addressed by TLS-only access, versioning, and a private-by-default policy,
# and the jobs bucket holds only short-lived job code/output behind the same
# TLS-only policy. Revisit for prod if compliance ever requires CMKs.
AVD-AWS-0132
